INT. LIGHTHOUSE KEEPER'S COTTAGE - SITTING ROOM - NIGHT

Low beams. A woodstove ticks. Salt-bleached curtains. Shelves of shells, charts, and mismatched teacups. Storm pushes at the windows.

INSPECTOR GWENDOLYN TREVELYAN-OKOYE, 50s, in a damp raincoat, stands by the stove. A younger officer, SERGEANT ABEL MWAMBA, 30s, takes notes in a corner. The keeper's widow, MRS. HEPZIBAH DRUMMOND, 70s, sits in a rocking chair with a shawl. Her son, WILFRED DRUMMOND, 40s, hovers by the doorway. A neighbor, MISS ODALYS PRENTISS, 60s, stirs tea at a side table.

GWENDOLYN
Mrs. Drummond, when did you last see your husband?

HEPZIBAH
Yesterday. At supper.

WILFRED
She means Tuesday.

HEPZIBAH
I mean yesterday.

WILFRED
It was Tuesday, Mother.

HEPZIBAH
Then it was Tuesday.

ABEL
(writing)
Tuesday supper.

ODALYS
I brought the pie.

GWENDOLYN
Which pie?

ODALYS
Gooseberry.

WILFRED
It's always gooseberry.

ODALYS
It's his favorite.

HEPZIBAH
It was his favorite.

A beat. The storm slaps the glass. Odalys sets down the spoon.

GWENDOLYN
Was there anything unusual about the evening?

HEPZIBAH
He was quiet.

WILFRED
He was always quiet.

HEPZIBAH
Not like that.

ODALYS
He mentioned the lamp.

GWENDOLYN
The lamp?

ODALYS
He said it would go out. Soon.

ABEL
It did go out.

WILFRED ^
It didn't go out. It was put out.

HEPZIBAH
Wilfred.

WILFRED
I'm only saying.

GWENDOLYN
Mr. Drummond, do you have something to tell me?

Wilfred looks at his mother. At Odalys. At the floor. The woodstove ticks. Hepzibah rocks slowly.

WILFRED
No.

GWENDOLYN
Mrs. Drummond?

HEPZIBAH
I think he does.

Wilfred swallows. Odalys looks at her hands. Abel's pen hovers above the page. Gwendolyn waits, patient as the sea.
